/*
================= TEST WRITING RULES =================

1. SECURITY TESTING
- Do NOT mock UserAuthorization or authorizeUser()
- Always use real SecurityContextHolder for authentication scenarios
- Simulate authentication explicitly:
    - No auth → set Authentication = null
    - Valid auth → set UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken
- ALWAYS mock userRepository.findByEmail(authentication.getName())
  to return a valid User when authentication is present
- Tests must fail at the security layer, NOT business logic
- Ensure security checks (authorizeUser / verifyOwnerAccess) are executed FIRST in flow

-----------------------------------------------------

2. MOCKING RULES
- Only mock repositories and external dependencies
- Never mock core business/security logic being tested
- If a method depends on repository calls BEFORE security,
  mock them to return valid data so execution reaches security
- Avoid unnecessary stubbing

-----------------------------------------------------

3. TEST FOCUS
- Each test must validate ONE behavior
- Ensure failure happens at the intended layer
- If testing security:
    → ALL deeper dependencies (category, food, etc.) must be valid/mocked
- Do not allow deeper logic (cart, address, category, etc.) to interfere

-----------------------------------------------------

4. DATA SETUP
- Provide valid data for all layers AFTER the one being tested
- NEVER leave repository calls returning null unless explicitly testing null behavior
- Always ensure:
    - restaurantRepository returns valid Restaurant
    - categoryRepository returns valid Category (if needed)
    - foodRepository returns valid Food (if needed)

-----------------------------------------------------

5. AUTHENTICATION SETUP (CRITICAL)
- Always use helper method like authenticateAs(email)
- authenticateAs MUST:
    - set SecurityContextHolder Authentication
    - mock userRepository.findByEmail(email) to return User
- Without this, authorizeUser() will fail or behave incorrectly

-----------------------------------------------------

6. ASSERTIONS
- Always assert the exact expected exception
- Do not allow unexpected exceptions (like NPE)
- If wrong exception occurs:
    → it means test setup is wrong, NOT business logic

-----------------------------------------------------

7. CLEANUP
- Always clear SecurityContextHolder after each test

=====================================================
*/